Z||000000||Back On Holiday||11-23-2001||08:02 PM||||Question, what brand shocks and struts does everybody recommend, and where can I find new ones for the best price?<P>I'm not sure yet wether or not I will need replacement, thought I'd do some homework first -ask the experts at the Forum!<BR><P>------------------<BR>84se Silver 2m4<BR>86se Silver 2m6||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||11-23-2001||08:04 PM||||I like my KYB GR2's there not the best, but I think the performance is great for the price.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||7
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||11-23-2001||09:50 PM|||| KYB's are the best for the buck. KONI's Are real expensive but best for racing applications. <P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||11-23-2001||11:27 PM||||Yup, for the money, KYB is hard to beat.  I've got GR-2 struts on the rear and Gas-A-Just shocks up front.  I would have liked Gas-A-Just all the way around, but it's not available.<P>Koni's better, but WAY more expensive.  You do get what you pay for, though.  If I could have afforded the Koni's I would have gone with them.  <P>Or, you can go with coil-overs from Held Motorsports...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Oh, my KYB's were purchased from <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tirerack.com</A>  and were ~$172 for all four, including taxes and shipping.  Hard to beat! <p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 11-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||4UMLA1||11-24-2001||08:29 PM||||If you are thinking about lowering your car, get Koni, they are the best!||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||11-24-2001||09:35 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  <BR>dba RD Enterprises<P>Got my Tokico's for my T-bird there, cheap.  They seel lots of other brands and springs cheap, too.<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Back On Holiday||11-25-2001||08:12 AM||||no, not going to lower car.  but cars left strut looks shot, leaning pretty badly, and the front two shock covers are rusted through.  I figured I'd replace them while I was ripping the front suspension apart and installing poly.  I won't be able to really drive this car til springtime so, what the hey...  Cause I didn't just to an almost entire rebuild on it and let the salty roads  mess up her nice looking body   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>PS thanks Formula88, I deliver alot of stuff from the Tirerack, i'll probably go there if I don't see anything better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Back On Holiday (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-25-2001||04:41 PM||||I just put Gabriel Pro Ryder Gas shocks and Gas Ryder struts (Both have Gabriel's VST valving) on mine for less than $150. They perform very well. I'm not sure if I hit a sale or if that was the regular price at pep boys. I'm more than happy with how it's improved the car. No more spine jaring whack going over railroad or speed bumps.<P>Just about anything you get will be better then the factory struts. Those appear to have only rebound damping and were not a gas unit as far as I know. (Bump damping is when you push the wheel up. Rebound is when it comes back down.)<P>I don't know what the factory shocks were. Mine had flat Monroe gas matics on it.<P>Between the new Gabriel shocks/struts and the OE sport springs I wouldn't want it any stiffer for a daily driver.<P>Also, the cam bolts for the struts are Monroe part AK-30 and are available thru PartsAmerica.com and affiliates for about $8 a set. I strongly recomend cam bolts if you don't have them or they are shot. Makes alignment go much faster and likely to be more accurate.<P>the PartsAmerica group also caries AmPro tools which makes a nice strut spring compressor for about $25-30. It has nice wide top and bottom jaws that won't slip or let the spring bow. Just let the compressor grab the sprin flat into the jaws. don't worry if the compressor is at an angle relative to the strut. (That applies to most bolt type compressors. They will generally tend to "home" themselves on the spring. fighting that tendancy is a sure way to make the compressors slip.)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-25-2001).]||||reg||1
